{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Learning Experience Design and Software Technology","title":"Feedback Loops and Learner Control","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting feedback loops and learner control to a feedback-loop specification (feed-forward text, reveal-timing rule, calibrated control, behavioral signal), reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"ail606-supplement-02-04-lo-01","statement":"Diagnose whether a practice loop feeds forward using Hattie and Timperley's three questions, and rewrite a bare verdict into actionable feedback"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-02-04-lo-02","statement":"Choose a feedback-reveal timing (immediate versus brief delay) justified by task type and learner expertise"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-02-04-lo-03","statement":"Design calibrated learner control that preserves autonomy while naming the cost of skipping"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-02-04-lo-04","statement":"Tie each loop revision to a behavioral signal such as repeat-error rate, skip rate, or retry uptake"}],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_learning_experience_design_and_software_technology","ail606_supplement_feedback_loops_and_learner_control","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":4,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"02","section":"04","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/02/03"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"ail606-supplement-02-04-lo-01":["ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_calibration","ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_artifact_signal"],"ail606-supplement-02-04-lo-02":["ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_ai_boundary"],"ail606-supplement-02-04-lo-03":["ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_annotation_signal"],"ail606-supplement-02-04-lo-04":["ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_calibration"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Feedback Loops and Learner Control\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Feedback Loops And Learner Control, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_learning_experience_design_and_software_technology\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/02-04-feedback-loops-and-learner-control.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Feedback Loops and Learner Control: Feedback Loops And Learner Control, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nTwo design controls quietly determine whether a learning activity actually teaches: the feedback the learner receives after acting, and the control the learner has over where to go next. Get the feedback wrong and the activity returns verdicts (\"Incorrect, 0/1\") that label performance without changing it. Get the control wrong and you either trap capable learners on material they have mastered or let struggling novices skip the scaffolding they most need. This section treats both as design variables you tune with evidence, not defaults you accept from the authoring tool.\n\nThe deeper idea is that feedback and control are coupled, not separate. A loop is only \"closed\" when feedback tells the learner what to do differently *and* the control structure actually lets them do it. Actionable feedback with no retry is a dead end; a retry with no actionable feedback is a guessing game. Designing the loop means designing the feedback content, its timing, and the control that follows it as one connected mechanism.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Diagnose whether a practice loop feeds forward using Hattie and Timperley's three questions, and rewrite a bare verdict into actionable feedback.\n- Choose a feedback-reveal timing (immediate versus brief delay) justified by task type and learner expertise.\n- Design calibrated learner control that preserves autonomy while naming the cost of skipping.\n- Tie each loop revision to a behavioral signal such as repeat-error rate, skip rate, or retry uptake.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA statistics module ships with what looks like a complete practice activity. Learners answer, the screen says \"Incorrect. Score: 0/1. Next,\" and a \"Skip remaining items\" link sits at the top for anyone who wants to move on. The screen is clean and the navigation is generous. Then the data lands: fourteen of twenty learners who saw the bare \"Incorrect\" chose the same wrong test on the very next item, and nine of twenty used the skip link before finishing the scaffolded examples. The activity was busy and free, and it taught almost nothing.\n\nThe case isolates the two failures. The feedback returned a verdict but no diagnosis, so learners repeated the same error with no way to correct it. The control was unbounded, so the learners who most needed the scaffolding were the ones who skipped it. Neither problem is visible by looking at the screen; both are visible only in the loop's behavior. The storyboard must record the loop's intended mechanism (what the feedback says, when the answer reveals, what skipping costs) so a reviewer can judge the loop, not just the layout.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nEffective feedback, in the research tradition of Hattie and Timperley, answers three questions for the learner: Where am I going (the goal), How am I doing (current state relative to the goal), and Where to next (the specific move that closes the gap). A verdict like \"Incorrect\" answers only the second, and badly. \"You chose an independent-samples test, but pre and post scores come from the same people, so the observations are paired\" answers all three, because it names the goal (match design to test), the error (treating paired data as independent), and the next move (try again with that mapping). The content of feedback, not its presence, is what makes a loop teach.\n\n**Timing** is a second design variable. Immediate feedback supports motor and simple-rule learning, but for tasks that build retrieval, a brief delay (one targeted retry before the answer is revealed) strengthens learning by forcing the learner to reconstruct rather than recognize. Revealing the answer instantly can feel helpful while quietly removing the effortful retrieval that produces durable memory.\n\n**Learner control** is the third variable, and it is a genuine trade-off rather than a value to maximize. More control supports motivation and autonomy, which self-determination theory treats as a driver of engagement. But unbounded control lets novices, who are the worst judges of what they need, skip the support that would help them most. The design answer is calibrated control: a recommended path is highlighted, skipping remains possible, but a skip is routed through a cue that names what the skipped section teaches.\n\nThe anchors keep these variables honest:\n\n- **Cognitive load theory** warns that elaborate feedback can itself overload; the diagnosis must be concise and tied to the specific error.\n- **UDL learner variability** reminds you that the right amount of control differs across learners, so a single fixed setting will fail someone.\n- **Mayer's principles** apply to the feedback display itself: avoid redundancy and keep the corrective cue signaled and close to the error.\n\nHattie and Timperley say effective feedback answers three questions. Match each question to the feedback move that answers it for the paired-data example.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Where am I going? (the goal)\",\"definition\":\"Naming the target: match the statistical design to the right test\"},{\"term\":\"How am I doing? (current state)\",\"definition\":\"Identifying the error: you treated paired observations as if they were independent\"},{\"term\":\"Where to next? (the move)\",\"definition\":\"The corrective step: try again, this time pairing the pre and post scores from the same people\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A practice item returns only 'Incorrect. Score: 0/1. Next.' Which of Hattie and Timperley's three questions does it answer?\" explanation=\"The verdict reports current state ('how am I doing') and even that only as a label. It names no goal and gives no corrective move, so it cannot feed forward. This is why 14 of 20 learners repeated the same wrong test: a verdict diagnoses nothing.\" options='[{\"text\":\"All three, because the score communicates everything the learner needs\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Only how-am-I-doing, and badly, since it labels the result without naming the goal or the next move\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Only where-to-next, because it says Next\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example: Redesigning a Feedback Loop\n\nHere is a before-and-after decision about the feedback and control on one practice item in a statistics module.\n\n**Before (loop v1)**\n\nAfter a learner selects an answer to \"Which test fits paired pre/post scores?\", the screen shows: \"Incorrect. Score: 0/1. Next.\" Control is wide open: a \"Skip remaining items\" link sits at the top.\n\n**The decision point.** Pilot data on 20 first-week learners showed:\n\n1. 14 of 20 who saw the bare \"Incorrect\" picked the same wrong test on the very next item.\n2. 9 of 20 used \"Skip remaining items\" before completing the scaffolded examples.\n\nThe loop was returning a verdict, not feedback, and the unbounded control let novices skip the support they most needed.\n\n**After (loop v2)**\n\n1. The verdict became actionable: \"Not quite. You chose an independent-samples test, but pre and post scores come from the same people, so the observations are paired. Try the item again, this time matching design to test.\"\n2. One targeted retry was added before the answer is shown, supporting retrieval rather than instant reveal.\n3. \"Skip remaining items\" was replaced with guided control: a recommended path is highlighted, and skipping is still possible but routed through a one-line \"are you sure\" that names what the skipped section teaches.\n\nDesigner note (decision level): \"I delayed the full answer because the task builds retrieval, and I converted free skipping to guided control because the pilot showed novices skipped scaffolding. Both choices trade a little learner freedom for a loop that actually closes.\"\n\nThe revision is defensible because each change is tied to pilot evidence (the 14-of-20 repeat rate, the 9-of-20 skip rate) and to a stated principle about feedback content, timing, and calibrated control.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nDesign the feedback content before the feedback styling, and decide the control structure from learner need rather than from the tool's default. A loop is justified only when it tells the learner what to do next and gives them the means to do it.\n\nBefore you call a loop finished, test it against these questions:\n\n1. **Feed-forward:** Does the feedback answer \"where to next?\" with a specific move, or does it only label the result?\n2. **Timing:** Is the answer revealed at the moment that helps learning (after a retrieval attempt), or instantly because that was easiest to build?\n3. **Calibrated control:** Can a learner skip, and if so, does the path make the recommended route obvious and name the cost of skipping?\n4. **Evidence:** Do you have (or can you collect) a behavioral signal, like next-item error rate or skip rate, that tells you the loop is closing?\n\nA loop that passes teaches. A loop that returns verdicts and offers unbounded skipping is, at best, an assessment, and a poor one.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading equates more feedback with better feedback, so designers pile on praise, explanations, and hints. Excess feedback overloads working memory and buries the one corrective move the learner needs. A short, specific feed-forward beats a paragraph of encouragement; concision is part of the design, not a shortcut.\n\nThe second misreading treats learner control as an unqualified good, so the most \"learner-centered\" design is assumed to be the one with the most freedom. The pilot in this section says otherwise: the learners who used the open skip link were disproportionately the novices who needed the scaffolding. Autonomy that lets a learner avoid the very support that would help is not respect, it is abandonment with a friendly label. Calibrated control, not maximal control, is the goal.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTrace the worked example as a re-engineering of a loop. The diagnosis comes from two behavioral signals: the 14-of-20 repeat-error rate exposes feedback that does not feed forward, and the 9-of-20 skip rate exposes control that is too open for novices. Each fix targets one signal. The verdict becomes a diagnosis (\"you chose independent-samples, but the scores are paired\") aimed squarely at the repeat error, which is Hattie and Timperley's \"where to next\" made concrete.\n\nNotice the timing change next. Adding one targeted retry *before* the answer is shown converts an instant reveal into a retrieval opportunity. This is a deliberate trade: the learner waits slightly longer for the answer, and in exchange the loop builds memory rather than recognition. The designer's note (\"I delayed the full answer because the task builds retrieval\") shows the reasoning is principled, not stylistic.\n\nFinally, watch control get calibrated rather than removed. Skipping is still possible, which preserves autonomy, but it is routed through a one-line \"are you sure\" that names what the skipped section teaches. That single sentence converts a blind exit into an informed choice, the smallest change that addresses the 9-of-20 problem without locking learners in. The walkthrough's lesson is that you tune feedback, timing, and control as three dials, each justified by a specific behavioral signal.\n\nWork the learner-control dilemma as a decision tree. The pilot shows 9 of 20 first-week novices used the open skip link before finishing the scaffolding.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Calibrating learner control\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Novices are skipping the scaffolding they most need. How do you adjust control?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Remove the skip link entirely to force completion\",\"next\":\"lock\",\"feedback\":\"This protects novices but strips the autonomy that supports motivation, and it traps experts who do not need the scaffolding.\"},{\"label\":\"Leave skipping fully open because more learner control is always better\",\"next\":\"open\",\"feedback\":\"The pilot already shows the most open setting fails the learners who need help most.\"},{\"label\":\"Keep skipping possible but route it through a cue that names what the skipped section teaches\",\"next\":\"calibrate\",\"feedback\":\"This is calibrated control: autonomy is preserved while a blind exit becomes an informed choice.\"}]},\"lock\":{\"prompt\":\"Completion rises, but advanced reviewers complain the module wastes their time and one novice reports feeling forced.\",\"feedback\":\"Maximal control and zero control both fail someone. The fix is to calibrate, not to eliminate, choice.\",\"choices\":[]},\"open\":{\"prompt\":\"The skip rate stays high and next-item error rates do not improve; the novices who skipped never met the scaffolding.\",\"feedback\":\"Autonomy that lets a learner avoid the help that would work is abandonment with a friendly label.\",\"choices\":[]},\"calibrate\":{\"prompt\":\"Skip rate among novices drops and those who still skip do so after seeing what they are giving up. Experts still move freely.\",\"feedback\":\"Calibrated control highlights the recommended path and names the cost of leaving it, addressing the 9-of-20 problem without locking anyone in.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, audit feedback you actually receive this week (from an app, a quiz, a fitness tracker) against Hattie and Timperley's three questions. You will find most feedback answers only \"how am I doing\" and skips \"where to next,\" which is exactly the gap that makes a verdict useless. Training your ear to hear the missing feed-forward is the core skill of this section.\n\nA strong reader of feedback loops and learner control can answer four questions about any practice activity:\n\n1. Does the feedback feed forward with a specific corrective move, or only label the result?\n2. Is the answer's timing tuned to the task (delayed for retrieval, immediate for simple rules)?\n3. Is control calibrated, so novices are guided but not trapped and experts are not held back?\n4. What behavioral signal (repeat-error rate, skip rate, retry uptake) would show the loop is actually closing?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong artifact for this section specifies the loop, not just the screen: the exact feedback text for at least one wrong answer (showing the feed-forward), the timing rule for revealing the answer, and the control structure with the cost of skipping made explicit. The strongest versions tie each of those three choices to a behavioral signal the designer would watch to confirm the loop closes.\n\nWhen you bring this to BigAL, ask it to write the feedback for a *specific* wrong answer rather than a generic one. A useful prompt is: \"A learner chose an independent-samples test for paired data. Write feedback that answers where they are going, how they are doing, and where to next, in under thirty words.\" Forcing specificity and a word limit exposes whether your loop actually feeds forward or just consoles.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same statistics loop from a self-paced module for first-week novices to a review activity for advanced students preparing for a qualifying exam. The feedback content can stay diagnostic, but the timing and control should change. For experts, the delayed reveal and the single targeted retry that helped novices may now slow them down without adding retrieval benefit, because they can already reconstruct the reasoning; immediate confirmation plus open navigation respects their expertise. The very calibrated control that protected novices becomes friction for experts. This is the [expertise-reversal effect](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expertise_reversal_effect): support that helps a novice can hinder an expert. If you can explain why the same loop should tighten control for novices and loosen it for experts, you understand feedback and control as variables tuned to the learner rather than as a single best design.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_feedback_loops_and_learner_control\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the feedback-loop specification packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/02-04-feedback-loops-and-learner-control-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"feedback-loop specification with feed-forward text, reveal-timing rule, calibrated control, and a behavioral signal\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"02.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the feedback-loop specification.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the feedback-loop specification for AIL 606 02.04. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_feedback_loops_and_learner_control\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the feedback-loop specification records the exact feedback text for at least one wrong answer (the feed-forward), the reveal-timing rule, the calibrated-control structure with the cost of skipping named, the behavioral signal you would watch to confirm the loop closes, and at least one rejected suggestion.\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_feedback_loops_and_learner_control","stem":"A quiz item responds to a wrong answer with only \"Incorrect. Score: 0/1.\" Why is this not yet a feedback loop?","options":["The score should be shown as a percentage instead of a fraction.","It delivers a verdict but no actionable information about what was misunderstood or what to try next.","It should award partial credit for effort.","The word Incorrect is too harsh and should be softened."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A verdict alone closes nothing. A feedback loop tells the learner what was misunderstood and gives a next move, so the learner can act.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_verdict_is_feedback","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_feedback_loops_and_learner_control"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-02-04-lo-01"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_feedback_loops_and_learner_control","stem":"A module for first-week novices adds a \"Skip any section\" menu giving full freedom to reorder and exit. What is the design risk?","options":["There is no risk; more control always improves motivation.","Novices often lack the prior knowledge to self-regulate, so unbounded skipping can let them miss the scaffolding they most need.","The menu will slow the page load.","Skipping is fine as long as a final exam blocks the exit."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Learner control should be calibrated to expertise. Guided control, a recommended path with optional detours, suits novices better than unbounded freedom.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_control_means_anything","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_feedback_loops_and_learner_control"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-02-04-lo-03"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_feedback_loops_and_learner_control","stem":"A vocabulary task is meant to build long-term retrieval. The designer attaches an instant answer reveal to every prompt. Why might delayed feedback serve this goal better?","options":["Delayed feedback is cheaper to build.","For retrieval and self-monitoring tasks, a brief delay forces the learner to commit and self-assess first, which can strengthen retention more than an instant reveal.","Instant feedback is never appropriate in any task.","Delayed feedback prevents cheating, which is the main concern."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Feedback timing is a design choice. Instant reveal can short-circuit the retrieval effort the task is built to produce.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_immediate_always_better","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_feedback_loops_and_learner_control"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-02-04-lo-02"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","stem":"Pilot data show 70 percent of learners who got the bare verdict \"Incorrect\" repeated the same error on the next attempt. Which revision is the strongest evidence-based response?","options":["Add an encouraging emoji to the Incorrect message.","Replace the bare verdict with feedback that names the specific error type and offers one targeted retry, then re-pilot to see whether the repeat-error rate drops.","Lower the passing threshold so fewer learners see Incorrect.","Hide the score so learners feel less discouraged."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The 70 percent repeat rate is evidence the loop is not closing. Actionable feedback plus a targeted retry, then re-measurement, is the defensible revision.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_verdict_is_feedback","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-02-04-lo-01","ail606-supplement-02-04-lo-04"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_verdict_is_feedback","pattern":"correctness_verdict_mistaken_for_feedback","description":"Treating a right or wrong verdict as instructional feedback rather than information the learner can act on","trigger":"learner designs a check that only says Correct or Incorrect with a score and no path to improve","feedback":"Feedback must be actionable. Beyond a verdict, tell the learner what was misunderstood and what to try next, or the loop teaches nothing.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_control_means_anything","pattern":"learner_control_as_unbounded_choice","description":"Equating learner control with unlimited freedom to skip, reorder, or exit, ignoring that novices often choose poorly","trigger":"learner gives full skip-everything control to beginners who lack the prior knowledge to self-regulate","feedback":"Learner control should be calibrated to expertise. Offer guided control (recommended path plus optional detours) for novices rather than unbounded freedom.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_4_feedback_loops_and_learner_control_immediate_always_better","pattern":"immediate_feedback_always_optimal","description":"Assuming immediate feedback is always superior, even when delayed feedback would better support retention or transfer","trigger":"learner attaches instant answer reveals to tasks meant to build retrieval or self-monitoring","feedback":"Feedback timing is a design choice. For tasks that build retrieval or self-assessment, brief delay can strengthen learning more than instant reveal.","rail_action":"represent"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"48fcef4bba0a5f41c92e49a1d4d77508ce0443689aa3d57f193e7e864c66faac","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}